Every nib loaded matrix lost its mode and all of its flags.

-[NSMatrix initWithCoder:] decodes NSMatrixFlags into a local in its KEYED branch, and then the
code that INTERPRETS that local sits inside the non keyed ELSE branch along with the selected cell
normalisation. So for every modern nib, which is every keyed archive, none of it runs:

  _mode                    never set, so it keeps the zero from alloc
  _allowsEmptySelection    never set
  _autosizesCells          never set
  _drawsBackground         never set
  _drawsCellBackground     never set
  _selectionByRect         never set
  _isAutoscroll            never set
  _tabKeyTraversesCells    never set
  _selectedIndex           never set, and -selectCell: is never called

The zero that _mode keeps happens to equal NSRadioModeMatrix, which is why this hid for so long:
every matrix in every application has been in radio mode by accident, and a matrix that really is a
radio group looked correct while a track mode or list mode one did not.

HOW IT SHOWS. A radio group draws TWO filled dots. Swift Publisher preferences has For New
Documents with Template Gallery and Blank Document both encoded on, and nothing normalises them
because the normalisation is in the branch that does not run:

  before   CIDER_MATRIX cells rows=3 cols=1 count=3 selected=0
             [0] state=1 title=Template Gallery
             [1] state=1 title=Blank Document
             [2] state=0 title=Radio
  after    [1] state=0

The fix is to move the shared block out of the else so it runs for both coders. The locals are
initialised too, because the non keyed branch can raise before setting either of them and the
shared block reads both.

WHAT THE TRACES SAID, and they are kept because this took several builds to corner. The decode
trace now reports real modes where before every matrix reported the same accidental zero:

  CIDER_MATRIX decode mode=0 cells=2 on=1     radio
  CIDER_MATRIX decode mode=1 cells=7 on=1     highlight
  CIDER_MATRIX decode mode=3 cells=4 on=1     track

CIDER_TRACE_CONTROL also dumps a matrix cell list at draw time with each title and state, which is
what separated a state defect from a drawing one, and CIDER_TRACE_CELLSTATE names who sets a cell
state with a backtrace. That one proved the two on cells were never sent setState at all, which is
what pointed at the decode rather than at the application.

The radio normalisation added next to the selected cell lookup is belt and braces: it adopts the
first cell that is on when NSSelectedCell is absent or names a cell that is not in NSCells. It did
NOT fire in any measured run here, because NSSelectedCell was present every time, and the hoist
alone is what fixed the defect above. It is kept because a radio matrix having at most one cell on
is an invariant the archive does not guarantee.

